NNAAMMEE
     rm - remove files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rrmm [-ffiirrttvv] _f_i_l_e ...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     rrmm removes each named _f_i_l_e. If a _f_i_l_e is the last link to
     its data, the space it occupies is freed.  Removing a direc-
     tory requires -rr, and requires write, execute, and search
     permission on the parent directory.

     Interrupts are caught while rrmm is working, so that a direc-
     tory removal in progress is not left in a half-finished
     state; the interrupt takes effect after the file or direc-
     tory currently being handled is finished.

     -ff   Force: remove a _f_i_l_e even without write permission on
          it, without asking, and do not report files that could
          not be removed.

     -ii   Interactive: ask, for each _f_i_l_e, whether to remove it;
          the file is removed only if the answer begins with yy or
          YY. -ii has no effect if the standard input is not a ter-
          minal.

     -rr   Recursive: if _f_i_l_e is a directory, rrmm first removes
          every entry within it (recursing into subdirectories),
          then removes the directory itself, provided it is not
          the caller's current directory or the root directory.
          Without -rr, naming a directory is an error.

     -tt   Test: perform all the same checks and prompts as a nor-
          mal run, but do not actually unlink anything.

     -vv   Verbose: after each _f_i_l_e is processed, report whether
          it was removed or not.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     llnn, rrmmddiirr

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     rrmm returns nonzero status if any _f_i_l_e could not be removed
     -- for example because it does not exist, the caller lacks
     permission, a directory argument was given without -rr, or a
     named directory turned out to be the caller's current direc-
     tory or the root directory.  Unless -ff is given, each such
     failure is also reported on the standard error.

NNOOTTEESS
     Without -ff or -ii, rrmm still asks for confirmation, on the
     standard error, before removing a file the caller lacks
     write permission on, reading the answer from the standard
     input; the file is removed only if the answer is yy. If the
     standard input is not a terminal, this prompt is preceded by


